hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1182","Bishr bin Muadh told us, Abu Awanah told us, on the authority of Ziyad bin Ulaqah, on the authority of Al-Mughirah bin Shu’bah, he said: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed. Until his feet swelled, and it was said to him: You did this, O Messenger of God, and you have been forgiven? He said: “Shall I not be a grateful servant?”"
"1183","Ali bin Khashram, Sa`id bin Abdul Rahman, and Abdul Jabbar bin Al Ala told us. Ali said: Ibn Uyaynah told us, and he said The other two: Sufyan narrated to us, on the authority of Ziyad bin Ulaqah, he heard Al-Mughirah bin Shu’bah say: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed until his feet swelled, so it was said To him: God has forgiven you for your past and future sins. He said: “Shall I not be a grateful servant?”"
"1184","Muhammad bin Ismail Al-Ahmasi told us, Abd Al-Rahman bin Muhammad Al-Muharbi told us, and Abu Ammar told us, Al-Fadl bin Musa told us. All of them, on the authority of Muhammad bin Amr, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, who said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to stand until his feet were wet. It was said to him: Yes, Messenger of God! Are you doing this when it has come to you from God that He has forgiven you for your previous and future sins? He said: “Shall I not be a grateful servant?” This is the word for a warrior. Abu Bakr said: This is an indication that gratitude to God Almighty may be done by doing work for Him, because all thanks are to God, and it may be with the tongue. God said, “Do to the family of David with gratitude.” Surat Sheba, verse 13. Then He commanded them, the Almighty and Majestic, to show gratitude to Him. Gratitude may be in word or deed in combination, not in words. As the common people imagine that gratitude can only be expressed with the tongue. And his saying: “May God forgive you for your past and future sins” is of the kind that I say is permissible in the language, it is said: to be in the meaning of It was, because God only said to His Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: Indeed, We have opened for you a clear conquest, Surat Al-Fath, verse 1, and it was said to the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: May God forgive you for your past and future sins. The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, did not respond to the speaker, nor did he say: He promised me that he would forgive. Because he has forgiven"
